\DOC LOCAL_MUX_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUX_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal of the form {eventually ... s_k} over a
block of machine instructions implementing a conditional select (multiplex) of
one bignum from two, replacing the state-describing assumptions with ones about
the new state and introducing a fresh variable equated to a conditional
expression.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes an {int} and a {string list}. The integer is an
offset/positional parameter selecting which instance of the block to step
through, which affects how many instructions are consumed and hence the
resulting program counter and state index. The string list names three
variables: the first names the freshly introduced result variable, and the
other two name the existing source values {t} and {u} that appear as the two
branches of the conditional. The resulting conclusion keeps the same
{eventually} predicate and post-condition, with only the current state variable
replaced by a later one. All assumptions describing the old state (program
counter, register and flag reads, memory contents, {bytes_loaded}, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s_old} frame) are discharged and replaced by
corresponding assumptions about the new state, the frame being extended by an
extra {MAYCHANGE} component for the memory region just written; assumptions not
mentioning the state (side conditions, non-overlapping facts, congruences, and
conditional definitions introduced by earlier applications) are retained. Two
further assumptions characterise the step: one equates the written memory
region in the new state to the fresh variable named by the first string, and
one defines that variable as {(if <condition on the selector> then u else t)}.
{
       A ?- eventually ... s_k
    ===========================================  LOCAL_MUX_4_TAC n ["x1"; "t"; "u"]
       A' ?- eventually ... s_m
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions re-stated at
the new state, plus the two new assumptions.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals of this symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
